package org.mattshoe.mtg.core

internal val setMarchOfTheMachine = StorySection(
    "mom", "March of the Machine",
    "Elesh Norn's tree broke into every world at once, and every world, from the greatest to the strangest, fought back.",
    listOf(
        StoryArticle(
            "mom-one-last-stand", "One Last Stand",
            listOf(
                StoryTab(
                    "mom-the-final-chapter", "The Final Chapter",
                    listOf(
                        "March of the Machine came out on April 21, 2023, under the set code MOM, after prerelease events that began on April 14 and a digital release on April 18. It was the ninety-sixth Magic expansion, and it was the last chapter of a story that had run across four sets. Dominaria United began it, The Brothers' War went back in time to the oil's first arrival, All Will Be One went down through the layers of New Phyrexia, and this set ended it. Its tagline was One Last Stand. Its expansion symbol is Elspeth's sword breaking a tilted Phyrexian sigil, a spoiler that sat in plain sight on every card in the set for weeks before the story was told.",
                        "It was not set on one world. Wizards called it an event set, and its stage was the whole Multiverse. [Elesh Norn](who/elesh-norn) had grown a tree that could reach any plane, and the cards follow her armies to more than thirty of them, from Ravnica and Dominaria to worlds nobody had visited in decades, Ulgrotha, Mercadia and Shandalar among them, and a handful nobody had ever seen before. Heroes who had never met fight side by side on single cards. The Phyrexians who come for them are often printed halfway through their change, flesh on one face and porcelain on the other.",
                        "A small set followed it on May 12, 2023. That was The Aftermath, code MAT, fifty cards sold in Epilogue Boosters, an epilogue for the days after the war, when most planeswalkers found their sparks gone and [the Omenpaths](the-omenpaths) had opened between worlds for anyone to walk. It has its own two tabs at the end of this page. Everything here is told from the official Magic Story episodes by K. Arsenault Rivera, the side stories that each followed a single plane, the flavor text on the cards and the Planeswalker's Guide published with the set. The wider shape of the war, as Lore tells it, is in [Invasion of the Multiverse](invasion-of-the-multiverse).",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "mom-before-the-storm", "Before the Storm",
                    listOf(
                        "The war began with a failure. Norn had grown a tree of her own in the Seedcore at the heart of New Phyrexia, from tyrite that the praetor Vorinclex had stolen from Kaldheim's World Tree. [Jace](who/jace) Beleren gathered a strike team to destroy it before it could spread. They carried a perfect copy of the Golgothian Sylex that Saheeli Rai had built, the same kind of bomb that once ended the Brothers' War, and they chose Elspeth Tirel to lead them because she had fought more wars against Phyrexia than anyone else alive.",
                        "New Phyrexia scattered them and ate them one at a time. [Nahiri](who/nahiri) was scratched in the first fight and the oil began to work on her. Lukka and Nissa Revane were lost in the Hunter Maze. Vraska was already too far gone when they found her, and she infected Jace. By the time the survivors reached the Seedcore the tree had broken through to other worlds. Kaya refused to set off the Sylex, afraid it would destroy every plane the tree touched. Jace tried anyway. Elspeth ran him through, took the bomb and planeswalked into the [Blind Eternities](the-blind-eternities), and it went off there. Nobody who saw it believed she had lived.",
                        "Kaya, Kaito Shizuki and Tyvar Kell were still in the Seedcore when the invasion began. Somewhere below the tree hung [Karn](who/karn), cut open and taken apart piece by piece, still alive and still able to speak. Two praetors had already crossed Norn and lost. Sheoldred had tried a coup and been captured. Urabrask had refused to serve and retreated into the depths of the Furnace Layer, leaving his host behind. Nothing on New Phyrexia stood between the Mother of Machines and the rest of the Multiverse, and she opened her portals.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "mom-the-invasion-tree", "The Invasion Tree",
                    listOf(
                        "Its defenders called it the Invasion Tree. The Phyrexians called it Realmbreaker, and to them it was a glorious symbol of their own inevitability. Grown from the stolen essence of the World Tree and twisted in the Seedcore into New Phyrexia's image, it rose through every layer of the plane, and its branches tore paths through the Blind Eternities to world after world. On its card, {Realmbreaker, the Invasion Tree} is a small artifact that drags lands out of an opponent's graveyard to its owner's side, and for ten mana it can be sacrificed to put every Praetor in a deck onto the battlefield at once.",
                        "Each branch ended in a three-pronged barb that opened when it struck the ground and anchored the branch in place. The branches were weapons in their own right. The Planeswalker's Guide says they crushed entire cities and pulled warships out of the sky with insultingly casual ease, and their barbs worked like talons, ripping into anything they could reach.",
                        "Thousands of spiny seedpods rode every branch across the void. Some carried a whole regiment of centurions, some a single hulking behemoth, and some held nothing but glistening oil, meant to burst high in a world's sky and rain phyresis down across it. A special pod called a cocoon took in someone already soaked in oil, sealed shut around them and replaced their flesh with machine. Converts made this way were called neophytes, and they were rough and crude, because the Machine Legion cared about speed and not elegance. {Converter Beast} shows the creatures that hunted for the cocoons, caging prisoners in their backs and bathing them in oil until they were ready.",
                        "There had been omens first. In the days before the invasion, the sign of Phyrexia began turning up where nobody had drawn it, in spilled ink, in clouds, in scrolls and running water, on plane after plane. {Portent Tracker} remembers it. Almost nobody understood what it meant until the skies turned red.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote("All worlds will know perfection.", "Elesh Norn, Breach the Multiverse"),
                ),
            ),
            listOf(
                StorySource("Set", "March of the Machine"),
                StorySource("Set", "March of the Machine: The Aftermath"),
                StorySource("Story", "Planeswalker's Guide to March of the Machine, by Emily Teng, on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Story", "March of the Machine, Episode 1: Triumph of the Fleshless, Magic Story on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki: March of the Machine"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki: Realmbreaker"),
            ),
        ),
        StoryArticle(
            "mom-the-machine-legion", "The Machine Legion",
            listOf(
                StoryTab(
                    "mom-the-alabaster-host", "The Alabaster Host",
                    listOf(
                        "Norn's army was called the Machine Legion, and it marched in five hosts, one for each of the praetors' colors. When an invasion began, its soldiers all came from New Phyrexia. As a world fell, more and more of the army came to be made of that world's own defenders, so every victory made the Legion larger, and every loss came back to fight against the living who were left.",
                        "The Alabaster Host was the largest of the five and the most fanatical, and it was found on every invaded plane. Its soldiers sang hymns to the Machine Orthodoxy as they marched, music that sounded terrible and discordant to everyone else and rapturous to themselves. [Elesh Norn](who/elesh-norn) led it directly, directing the whole invasion from her throne room on New Phyrexia. Cards place it on Arcavios, Kamigawa, Kaladesh, Eldraine, Theros, Zendikar, Gobakhan and Tolvada. {Norn's Inquisitor} is one of its soldiers, and {Alabaster Host Sanctifier} one of its priests, preaching that Heliod's light would scour away every shadow of doubt.",
                        "On Theros the host had a regent, Cymede, once a queen of Akros, compleated and carried into the sky on {Phyrexian Pegasus}. Its work there was the most frightening of the whole war. Gods on Theros are shaped by what their people believe, and so the Alabaster Host struck first at temples and places of worship. Every worshipper it compleated pulled a god a little further toward Phyrexia, until the gods themselves gave way.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "mom-chrome-and-etched", "Chrome and Etched",
                    listOf(
                        "The Chrome Host belonged to Jin-Gitaxias. He had been the first praetor to side with Norn in her civil war, and his reward was to keep command of his own host. He used it to gather knowledge across the Multiverse, sharing some of what he learned with Norn and keeping the rest. Each plane got the Chrome Host in whatever shape his existing intelligence about that plane suggested. Where his knowledge was good, it struck with surgical precision. Where it was wrong or out of date, it found itself badly outmatched, and on Kamigawa, the card {Invasion of Kamigawa} notes, the plane had changed so fast that his reports were already stale when the invaders arrived. On Kaladesh the host went straight for the Aetherflux Reservoir, wanting the plane's aether, and {Chrome Host Seedshark} is one of its creatures.",
                        "The Etched Host had been Sheoldred's. After her failed coup, Norn divided it among the remaining Steel Thanes and promised power and glory to whichever of them did best. The competition made it the most vicious and brutal host of the five, each thane driving its soldiers to deeper cruelty to win Norn's approval. It went to Dominaria, Innistrad, Ixalan, Kaldheim, Kaladesh, Kamigawa, Lorwyn, Arcavios and Capenna. {Ichor Drinker} is one of its soldiers.",
                        "Some of its work is remembered in single lines of flavor. On Lorwyn, {Compleated Huntmaster} says, the elves had always sought perfection, and the Etched Host supplied it. On Tolvada, oil seeped into the funeral shrouds of the dead and raised them as {Ichor Shade} to fight for the invaders. Where the Alabaster Host preached, the Etched Host simply competed to see who could be worst.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "mom-furnace-and-copper", "Furnace and Copper",
                    listOf(
                        "The Furnace Host had no master at all. Urabrask would not bow to Norn, and rather than lead his host for her he abandoned it and went into hiding. Norn did not appoint anyone in his place. She simply turned the Furnace Host loose on the Multiverse with no plans and no orders, to wreak whatever havoc it pleased. The result was chaotic, and it worked. {Thrashing Frontliner} is one of those masterless soldiers. On Kamigawa the host let willing akki converts experiment however they liked, and on Eldraine it took Ayara, the queen of Locthwain, whose compleated face is the back of {Ayara, Widow of the Realm}. {Furnace Gremlin} shows how far down the host's ranks the chaos ran.",
                        "The Copper Host was Vorinclex's. He had accepted Norn's rule for the moment and would turn on her at the first sign of weakness, and she knew it. He used the invasion as a test. Anyone strong enough to deserve Phyrexia was compleated, and anyone weak was torn apart, so the Copper Host compleated fewer people than any other host, and the ones it did take were among the most savage converts in the whole war.",
                        "It terraformed Amonkhet's dead desert with Phyrexian growths and stormed Ikoria with monsters as big as Ikoria's own. On Ikoria it brought down walls built to stop the plane's apex predators, as {Copper Host Crusher} shows. In Kaldheim's Cosmos it found Koma, the Cosmos Serpent, and made it Phyrexian, which is the moment of {Cosmic Hunger}. The Copper Host sought only the strongest converts, the card says, and in Koma it found perfection.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "mom-the-evangels", "The Evangels",
                    listOf(
                        "Above the hosts stood Norn's evangels, the planeswalkers Phyrexia had compleated. On Kamigawa, Jin-Gitaxias had learned to compleat a planeswalker without destroying the spark, so they kept their power to cross between worlds, and Norn used it. She sent each of them home to conquer the place they came from. [Nahiri](who/nahiri) went to Zendikar, Lukka to Ikoria, Tamiyo to Kamigawa and Vraska to Ravnica. [Jace](who/jace) was sent to Vryn, the world he was born on.",
                        "Ajani Goldmane, compleated in secret long before on Dominaria, was not sent home to Alara. Norn sent him to Theros, with priests and the Argent Etchings, to compleat its gods through their faithful. Atraxa, an angel remade by the hands of four praetors, with Norn keeping her heart, went to Capenna. Phyrexians had been to Capenna once before, ages ago, and its angels had sealed them away by giving up their own bodies. Norn told Atraxa to find the source of the Halo those angels left behind and destroy it, and she told her there was a third reason too. A danger to New Phyrexia was tied to Capenna and to Theros both. She did not say that the danger was a woman in white who walked through her nightmares.",
                        "Nissa Revane stayed at Norn's side. Compleated in the Hunter Maze, she had driven Realmbreaker to its full size, and through it she could turn the tree's attention to any world Norn asked to see. On Dominaria, the war was led by Rona, a convert at last made whole, whose card {Rona, Herald of Invasion} transforms into the Tolarian Obliterator that fell on Tolaria West and its students.",
                    ),
                ),
            ),
            listOf(
                StorySource("Set", "March of the Machine"),
                StorySource("Story", "Planeswalker's Guide to March of the Machine, by Emily Teng, on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Story", "March of the Machine, Episode 1: Triumph of the Fleshless, Magic Story on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki: New Phyrexia's Invasion of the Multiverse"),
                StorySource("Reference", "Scryfall"),
            ),
        ),
        StoryArticle(
            "mom-the-invasion-begins", "The Invasion Begins",
            listOf(
                StoryTab(
                    "mom-sheoldreds-end", "Sheoldred's End",
                    listOf(
                        "The story opens in Norn's sanctum, and Norn is the one telling it. Three prisoners stand before her, Kaya, Kaito and Tyvar, survivors of the strike team, and she does not bother to offer them compleation, certain they would refuse. Around her throne five portals look out on five worlds whose skies have turned red. Realmbreaker's barbed limbs anchor themselves wherever they please, rivers of oil run onto the earth, and seedpods fly from the barbs in perfect unison. To [Elesh Norn](who/elesh-norn), thousands of mouths speaking with one voice is the only beauty there is.",
                        "Then her evangels carried in Sheoldred, bound, stripped of her armor and small. Sheoldred told Norn that she had never cared about unity, only about herself. Norn answered that Phyrexia no longer had any place for those who craved power over unity, and ordered Ajani to execute her. His axe fell, Sheoldred's head rolled to Norn's feet, and her body was sent away for processing, because nothing in Phyrexia goes to waste.",
                        "Norn let the three prisoners escape on purpose, to carry word of Phyrexia's coming to everyone else. Then she sent her evangels out one by one, asking each where they were born while Nissa turned the portals to show the place. [Nahiri](who/nahiri) asked for a Skyclave, an ancient weapon of the kor that she could wake again. Lukka promised to bring Ikoria to heel through its monsters, and Tamiyo guessed aloud that he would die there. Tamiyo herself watched a mother on Kamigawa run from falling tiles with a child under each arm, and hesitated, and Atraxa had to cut through her silence. Ajani saw a god of Theros defending a temple and guessed that Norn was after the gods, though her real reason for sending him was one she kept to herself.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "mom-the-safe-house", "The Safe House",
                    listOf(
                        "On Dominaria, [Chandra](who/chandra) Nalaar was waiting in one of [Liliana](who/liliana) Vess's warded cabins with Liliana, Vivien Reid, and the dryad Wrenn with her tree companion Seven. The plan was simple. If two weeks passed with no word from the strike team, they would assume everyone was dead. Chandra spent the days teaching Wrenn to breathe through the fire that lived inside her, the way Jaya Ballard had once taught Chandra, though Jaya was dead now, killed by the compleated Ajani.",
                        "Kaya, Kaito and Tyvar came back with the worst news there was. Nissa had been taken. [Jace](who/jace) had become one of them. Nahiri had fallen, and Elspeth had run Jace through and vanished into the Blind Eternities with the Sylex. The others meant to go home and defend their own worlds. Chandra wanted to go back to New Phyrexia and bring down the tree, and Kaya told her plainly that she would only come back compleated. Liliana, hurt by the news about Jace more than she would show, left to warn Strixhaven.",
                        "Chandra walked out alone, and Wrenn followed her. The dryad had heard the tree's song all the way from Dominaria, distant and pained, a howl without melody, and she believed she could bond with it and turn it. She believed they could find [Teferi](who/teferi), lost somewhere in a tangle of time she had been studying from the cabin. She could not do it alone. It would take fire, and it would take the tree itself. Unseen watchers looked on as the two of them left, and one of them said they were there to witness the beginning of the end.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote("The rest of the Gatewatch retreated. Chandra and Wrenn went in blazing.", "Into the Fire"),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "mom-boseiju-falls", "Boseiju Falls",
                    listOf(
                        "Tamiyo came to Kamigawa reading a story. Years before, she had written down the history of Mirrodin and Phyrexia, and sealed the scroll with an iron ring because of what the story did to her mind. Now, floating over Towashi, she read it aloud, and every syllable shook the city. Trains left their tracks, the earth split, and the great tree Boseiju burst apart and poured oil like wine from a broken cask. The kami who lived in it dissolved into mist.",
                        "Kaya came to help Kaito, and saved a boy and his dog from a falling apartment tower. Kaito went up the tree after Tamiyo, and with him went a small nezumi in homemade armor. It was Nashi, Tamiyo's adopted son, who believed his mother would listen to him. She did not. She attacked them both, and offered Kaito one last chance to come home to Phyrexia.",
                        "The Wanderer arrived on the guardian kami Kyodai and struck Tamiyo down with a single cut. She said afterwards that Tamiyo had been asking for it. Every shard Tamiyo threw at her had gone wide, and before the blow Tamiyo had mouthed a request. Then a figure of glowing characters rose from her scrolls. It was her story unending, a memory of herself that Tamiyo had written years ago and sealed away against the day she died. It went to Nashi, and in the last episode the story says the boy's father knew her the moment he heard her voice.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote("With one swift, merciful stroke, Tamiyo's story came to an end.", "Cut Short"),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "mom-kaldheim-and-kaladesh", "Kaldheim and Kaladesh",
                    listOf(
                        "Tyvar Kell came home to Kaldheim to find the corrupted tree strangling the World Tree and seedpods falling like shed scales. He fought beside his brother Harald, king of the elves of Skemfar, and when a Doomskar opened beneath them he held the ground together with his own magic while the elves climbed onto Omenseeker longboats. Dwarves, humans, fire giants, trolls and the dead sailed together, old grudges set aside until the morrow, through the Cosmos toward the Invasion Tree. When a compleated sea serpent rose and bit a longboat in half, Tyvar leapt from the ship into its mouth.",
                        "In the end Kaldheim's warriors burned their own World Tree rather than let Phyrexia have it. The god Esika, already wounded by Vorinclex a year before, lost her sense of the Cosmos when the corrupted tree broke into it, and the mortal Inga Rune-Eyes had to chart her chariot's path for her.",
                        "On Kaladesh, Pia Nalaar had been warned by Saheeli Rai a week before and had started seeing the Phyrexian sign in spilled ink. Ghirapur was evacuated, and the artisans worked without sleep on Saheeli's Project Golden Scale. When the portals opened, bronze dinosaurs and pterodons rose from hatches in the streets to meet the invaders, built after creatures Saheeli had seen on Ixalan. The Phyrexians made for the Aetherflux Reservoir, which would take the whole city with it if it were breached. Pia, whose pilot was killed by a quill in midair, found herself flying a stolen skimmer she had never flown before, thinking about the tea she was due to share next month with her daughter, [Chandra](who/chandra).",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote("The warriors of Kaldheim burned the soul of their world to keep it out of Phyrexia's hands.", "Invasion of Kaldheim"),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "mom-atraxa-in-capenna", "Atraxa in New Capenna",
                    listOf(
                        "Atraxa found New Capenna an affront to everything she was. Her orders were to harvest it, every living body taken apart for parts, no prisoners. She took the Maestros into the Machine Legion, and through their minds she kept meeting a word she did not understand. Beautiful. To find where it came from, she went into a museum and destroyed everything inside, the paintings, the statues, and at last a room that displayed the remains of the Phyrexians who had come before her, which the Capennans had stood around with drinks, joking about how much the old armor might be worth.",
                        "Outside the museum stood the stone seraphs of the cathedral, the angels who had sealed away the first invaders. Atraxa knew at once that they were beautiful, and hated them more than she had ever hated anything. She struck their heads off and kept striking until nothing was left of them but dust, though the Halo rising from the stone seared her carapace. Then she went back to the war.",
                        "The seraphs did not stop her. A visitor was among them, and asked why they let her go. It was not yet time, the angels answered, and the visitor would know what to do when the end came. Elsewhere, Elspeth Tirel was about to wake up.",
                    ),
                ),
            ),
            listOf(
                StorySource("Set", "March of the Machine"),
                StorySource("Story", "March of the Machine, Episode 1: Triumph of the Fleshless, Magic Story on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Story", "March of the Machine, Episode 2: Holding Your Breath, Magic Story on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Story", "March of the Machine, Episode 3: Mother, Son, and Story, Magic Story on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Story", "March of the Machine, Episode 4: Beneath Eyes Unblinking, Magic Story on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki: New Phyrexia's Invasion of the Multiverse"),
            ),
        ),
        StoryArticle(
            "mom-into-the-seedcore", "Into the Seedcore",
            listOf(
                StoryTab(
                    "mom-koth-and-urabrask", "Koth and Urabrask",
                    listOf(
                        "[Chandra](who/chandra) and Wrenn landed on New Phyrexia beneath a tree so tall that Chandra could not see its crown. At the foot of a broken spire they found the last of the Mirran resistance, led by Koth of the Hammer, with Melira beside him, the girl born without metal who could not be compleated. Koth was not glad to see only two planeswalkers. Wrenn told him her plan. She would bond with the tree and guide it. Chandra told him a friend was coming too, the greatest time mage who had ever lived.",
                        "Koth had a powerful friend of his own, if friend was the word. Urabrask, the red praetor, had refused Norn and hidden in the Furnace Layer, and he despised her for stifling the fires of creation with her preaching. Phyrexia could not thrive, he said, if there was only one Phyrexia. He showed them hidden ways past Norn's patrols and carved runes into the metal of a platform. The plan was that the Mirrans would pick a fight with a patrol somewhere to draw Norn's attention, and Koth would launch Chandra and Wrenn across the chasm onto the tree.",
                        "It almost worked. They landed hard on one of Realmbreaker's lower branches, fought off a swarm of bladed fliers, and Wrenn began to reach into the tree. Then the white plates of the branch shifted and formed into centurions, and the centurions all spoke with one woman's voice. They did not want to hurt them, the voice said. They only wanted to welcome them home. [Norn](who/elesh-norn) had known about the plan all along.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote("\"I have a reckless idea,\" Koth offered. Chandra grinned and cracked her knuckles.", "Coming In Hot"),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "mom-on-the-branch", "On the Branch",
                    listOf(
                        "The voice was Nissa's. She had four arms now, copper roots where flesh had been, and black tears streaked down her cheeks. She cut Seven apart limb by limb and let what was left of Wrenn fall onto the branch. Spears pinned Chandra to the tree. Nissa told her she no longer felt any pain, and that her new form must be hard for Chandra to understand, words that are printed on {Traumatic Revelation}.",
                        "Chandra could have burned the whole branch, and Nissa with it. She would not do it. Nissa showed her the army waiting across the bridge, a numberless host with a draconic monstrosity carrying a blue banner at its head, and told her the resistance had never had a chance. She had asked the Mother of Machines for permission to speak with Chandra herself, because she knew what Chandra could become if she joined them. Then, for a moment, the voice was only Nissa's. She held out her hand and said that she missed her.",
                        "Chandra said she missed Nissa too, so much, and that she could not. The softness left Nissa's face. Chandra ducked under her blade and drove a burning fist into the branch, and the explosion threw them both away from the tree. Only Wrenn, already halfway grafted to it, held on. Wrenn would have to handle it from there.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote("\"My new form must be hard to understand.\"", "Traumatic Revelation"),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "mom-elspeths-choice", "Elspeth's Choice",
                    listOf(
                        "Elspeth woke in a dungeon near the home of her childhood, on the world now called Capenna, with eyes she did not remember having, and killed the beast that once killed a girl's mother while the girl watched. The girl was herself. A woman's voice told her she had a choice to make, and that she was becoming something new. Feathers drifted past her. She did not know whether she had died when the Sylex went off.",
                        "The voice showed her where she might intervene. New Capenna, burning under Atraxa, the city her family had once called home. Theros, where Daxos waited for her, and where Ajani stood on Heliod's altar forcing a priestess to drink oil, three gods already compleated. She turned both down. New Capenna had defenders of its own, and saving Ajani would save only one person.",
                        "Last she saw the Seedcore. Norn on her throne, Urabrask stretched on a rack and carried away in quarters, Karn's ruined body hanging over the bridge, and Koth, Wrenn and a broken Chandra thrown down before [Norn](who/elesh-norn) while Nissa called them traitors. Time stopped as Jin-Gitaxias raised his claws. The woman revealed herself as Serra, who long ago made a peaceful plane of angels and never imagined the threat that would follow the wizard [Urza](who/urza) when he came to her for help. Elspeth understood that her task was not to kill Norn. It was to hold Nissa back long enough for Wrenn to reach the tree.",
                        "She said she was afraid. Serra told her fear was always the last thing to leave, and remade her as an archangel. When time moved again, Elspeth's sword was there to meet Jin-Gitaxias's claws. The card {Archangel Elspeth} shows what she became.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote("On the precipice of eternity, Elspeth made a choice. The fight would not end without her.", "Moment of Truth"),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "mom-divine-intervention", "Divine Intervention",
                    listOf(
                        "[Karn](who/karn) saw her come. He hung over the bridge on a slab of the same metal that had once stopped him planeswalking in the Caves of Koilos, most of his body taken for scrap, holding on to his pain because it was the last thing still his. Urza had told him once that he could feel pain because people are less likely to hurt something that screams. He had closed his eyes to wait for the end when the golden light came.",
                        "Elspeth landed in a crater of her own making and sent dozens of Jin-Gitaxias's soldiers into the abyss. She healed Chandra and then Koth with a touch. Norn screamed at her and flung pieces of her own throne. Koth split the bridge with magma and called Mirrodin to him, and the Mirrans ran for the tree, Chandra carrying what was left of Wrenn and Koth flying Karn's slab along behind them as a shield.",
                        "Norn wove herself armor out of the bodies of the dead and tempted Elspeth the way she tempted everyone, with a home, with friends, with endless peace. Elspeth admitted they were not so different. Then Jin-Gitaxias told Norn to stop talking and compleat her, and Norn tore into him, and that told Elspeth everything. Phyrexia meant nothing to Norn, she said, only power did. Norn screamed that she hated her. Elspeth broke away and flew for the tree as Jin-Gitaxias's legions charged the Mirrans, and Melira fell.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "mom-wrenn-and-eight", "Wrenn and Eight",
                    listOf(
                        "Wrenn was mostly gone by then, only a head and a torso carried in Chandra's arms. She was not afraid of dying. She was afraid the tree would refuse her. Nissa caught Chandra with a brass root and hoisted her into the air by the throat, and the Mirrans passed Wrenn hand to hand toward the tree, falling one after another as they did. Melira, wounded through the stomach, carried her the last steps, set her against Realmbreaker's plating and gave her all the strength she had left.",
                        "Inside the tree there was no song, only oil, and a thousand voices telling her she did not belong. Wrenn's fire fought them and began to burn her too. Then Elspeth struck Nissa from behind in a blaze of gold, and Chandra fell free and laid her hand on Wrenn's shoulder and took the fire through herself, as she had once tried to teach her on Dominaria. Fire would burn no matter what, Chandra told her, but it could be shaped.",
                        "Deep in the dark, Wrenn found a sapling, small and frightened and pale, the tree's true self. She sang to it and made it grow, and called it Eight. Together, as {Wrenn and Realmbreaker}, they looked through every world the branches touched, and then got lost on purpose, following [Teferi](who/teferi)'s tangled trail to a place that time could not touch. It was Zhalfir, the plane that had been phased out of the Multiverse centuries ago, and Teferi stood beside its queen, more at ease than Wrenn had ever seen him.",
                        "A whole army could not pass through one leaf of the tree, so Wrenn chose something larger. She and Eight would push the planes against each other until New Phyrexia gave way, and leave Zhalfir in its place. Neither of them would survive it. Teferi called, and thousands answered. The portal that opened was greater than a mountain, and the Phyrexian army that had just thrown Elspeth through Koth's barricade found itself staring into a green and living world, with a mage at the head of an army walking out of it.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote("Wrenn reached out to Teferi through the Blind Eternities, and an impossible connection flared across the vast emptiness.", "Transcendent Message"),
                ),
            ),
            listOf(
                StorySource("Set", "March of the Machine"),
                StorySource("Story", "March of the Machine, Episode 5: Cathartic Reunion, Magic Story on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Story", "March of the Machine, Episode 6: The Last to Leave, Magic Story on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Story", "March of the Machine, Episode 7: Divine Intervention, Magic Story on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Story", "March of the Machine, Episode 8: Wrenn and Eight, Magic Story on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki: Wrenn"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki: Elspeth Tirel"),
            ),
        ),
        StoryArticle(
            "mom-the-fall-of-phyrexia", "The Fall of Phyrexia",
            listOf(
                StoryTab(
                    "mom-the-angels-go-out", "The Angels Go Out",
                    listOf(
                        "On New Capenna the stone angels woke. They had waited centuries for a call to serve again, and they answered it, shielding the city's people while demons and devils, the angels' oldest rivals, took the offensive beside them. The youngest was Giada, too new to fight, who watched from the towers and called out where help was needed. Atraxa cut through Park Heights with an army ten thousand strong. The Riveteers, too small and too many for her to notice, cut the tower loose from its supports, and the city fell on her. The resistance called the plan Operation Falling Star, and the street artist Errant placed the charges with Giada beside her the whole way, unseen, the pair on {Errant and Giada}.",
                        "With Atraxa dead, the branches over Capenna began to pull back, and the angels went out through them to other worlds. On Theros, where no one had ever seen an angel, they shielded the faithful in the temples and held sea monsters still while sailors cut off their heads. While the compleated Heliod blazed at them, Kaya climbed onto his back and drove a dagger into his throat, and the angels kept his oil off her as he died.",
                        "Angels poured out onto every world that needed them, worlds that worshipped them and worlds that hated them. The ones who went to New Phyrexia knew they would never come back, and they went anyway. Giada called to Elspeth through a portal, told her she was happy to see her, and told her help was coming.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote("\"Fallen angels should stay fallen.\"", "Henzie \"Toolbox\" Torre, Atraxa's Fall"),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "mom-zhalfir-charges", "Zhalfir Charges",
                    listOf(
                        "Zhalfirins say that they know fear better than anyone, and that a fear you invite into your house can no longer frighten you. They poured through the portal smiling, warclans armed to the teeth, their sidars leading a chant about how nothing woven together can ever be broken. Healers tended the wounded Mirrans on the platform while the army drove a wedge into the Phyrexian ranks with [Teferi](who/teferi) riding at its head, and every weapon thrown at him slowed to a crawl as it came near.",
                        "Vorinclex leapt at the vanguard and found himself moving through slowed time, until he forced his way out of it, tore the jaws from Teferi's mount and pinned the mage down. Teferi told him to look behind him. An Askari warrior named Shella took the praetor's head off with a blazing sword, helped Teferi to his feet and went straight back to work. Over all of them, New Phyrexia was cracking apart as the planes moved, slabs of metal raining down on both armies.",
                        "[Norn](who/elesh-norn) came on, her armor broken open, screaming that what she had built would last forever and demanding to know why no one was protecting her. Jin-Gitaxias answered from a war machine that carried a vat of his own unborn progeny. Her ego, he told her, was a tumor on whatever talent she had, and he turned his legions on her. Ajani, badly wounded, leapt to defend her and was taken alive on Teferi's order. Teferi stopped the machine's spinning blades with his hand for one second, and a Zhalfirin warrior brought her warhammer down on the vat. Jin-Gitaxias fell into it, and his own creations began to eat him.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote("\"Let's show these soulless fiends what a real army looks like! Forward!\"", "Shella, knight of Zhalfir, Inspired Charge"),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "mom-karn-and-norn", "Karn and Norn",
                    listOf(
                        "Teferi went back to the platform where Wrenn had become a statue of ash jutting from the tree, and found an acorn hidden in the ash, and put it in his pocket. Nissa came at them one more time. Chandra stepped in front of her with open arms, sure that Nissa would never kill her, and Elspeth struck Nissa on the back of the head with the pommel of her golden sword. Nissa dropped out of her armor and out of the sky, and Chandra caught her.",
                        "[Karn](who/karn) asked Teferi for one more moment, so that he could walk out of New Phyrexia under his own power, and built himself a rough new body while Teferi held time still. Then he looked at what was crawling toward the portal. [Norn](who/elesh-norn)'s own army had taken her legs and torn away her skin. Even her headpiece was shattered, and still she dragged herself through the dead toward Zhalfir.",
                        "Karn had sworn long ago never to harm a living thing. He had broken that vow before, in the first Phyrexian war, and hated it every time. He knew Norn might die of her wounds, and he knew he could not be sure she would. He laid a hand on what was left of her head and took her apart with his magic, tearing porcelain from wire, forcing himself to watch, because asking anyone else to do it would have been the same thing. Mirrodin had been his creation, and the oil had come from him. When it was done he walked through the portal, and Zhalfir welcomed him.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote("\"You were my mistake. I will forever bear the weight of your horrors, and of your end.\"", "Mirrodin Avenged"),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "mom-the-rhythms-of-life", "The Rhythms of Life",
                    listOf(
                        "All across the Multiverse, at the same moment, the Phyrexians stopped. On Kaladesh, Pia Nalaar was cornered above the Aetherflux Reservoir with a scrap of metal for a weapon when the soldiers around her crumbled like twigs. On Kaldheim, Harald counted to three hundred and thirteen before Tyvar burst out of the sea holding the serpent's head. Some invaders froze, some fell apart, and those already compleated dropped as if into a deep sleep. Saheeli Rai guessed afterwards that the oil had carried Norn's signal, and with New Phyrexia gone and Norn dead, there was nothing left to give it orders. That is the moment of {Render Inert}.",
                        "On Zhalfir the drums said rejoice. [Teferi](who/teferi) had been gone for centuries, and to his people it had been a short while. He planted Wrenn's acorn on a hill above a village, gave it water from his gourd, and wept for his lost years, for Karn's past, and for everyone who could not join the dancing by the fire. Mirrodin's five suns had followed the survivors across and now turned in Zhalfir's sky, but only a handful of Mirrans had made it through.",
                        "Melira was dying of her wound, and she had one last idea. Nissa and Ajani lay asleep with Phyrexian metal still in them. Teferi stopped time around them all. Karn drew out their sparks and passed them through the spark Venser had given his life to put in him, and spent it. Melira cleansed them, Kaya helped push them back into their bodies, and Melira died in Koth's arms.",
                        "Ajani woke, asked where he was, and slept again. Nissa's spark had flickered and crumbled in the process, and for a long moment she did not move. Then she said [Chandra](who/chandra)'s name. Chandra held her and kissed her and promised she was right there and was not going anywhere. Karn said only that he felt lonelier. What came after, the sparks going out and the doors opening between worlds, is told in [Sparks Lost](sparks-lost) and [the Omenpaths](the-omenpaths).",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote("Teferi gave them time. Karn gave them a chance. But it was Melira who paid the ultimate price to free Ajani from Phyrexia's grasp.", "Negate, March of the Machine"),
                ),
            ),
            listOf(
                StorySource("Set", "March of the Machine"),
                StorySource("Story", "March of the Machine, Episode 9: The Old Sins of New Phyrexia, Magic Story on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Story", "March of the Machine, Episode 10: The Rhythms of Life, Magic Story on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Story", "New Capenna: The Fall of Park Heights, by Elise Kova, Magic Story on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki: New Phyrexia-Zhalfir switch"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki: Melira"),
            ),
        ),
        StoryArticle(
            "mom-planes-at-war", "Planes at War",
            listOf(
                StoryTab(
                    "mom-strixhavens-last-spell", "Strixhaven's Last Spell",
                    listOf(
                        "[Liliana](who/liliana) Vess warned Strixhaven, and the university on Arcavios readied its strongest spellcasters and most powerful spells. The invaders were ready for them. The faculty formed the first line of defense, and most of them fell and were compleated and turned on their own students. {Phyrexian Censor} remembers what had been Professor Pitnik confiscating one priceless book after another while the student Quintorius Kand muffled a sob and watched.",
                        "The students' only hope was a fabled ancient spell hidden deep in the Biblioplex, and to reach it they had to creep through its halls while their own professors hunted them. Quintorius, Rootha, Dina and Zimone found it. Its card is {Invasion of Arcavios}, whose other face is the Invocation of the Founders. When it went off, the Biblioplex buckled and fell, the stones of the outlying buildings came apart like sugar in tea, the portals crumpled, and the tree's branches thrashed as the sky tried to close over them.",
                        "Strixhaven was a ruin, but the main wave of compleated professors was gone. The survivors regrouped under Professor Vess and Dean Embrose Lu. When the students who had cast the spell were dug out of the rubble, Quintorius was missing. The strain of the casting had ignited his spark, and he was gone. Zimone and Dina, friends who had never worked on a project together, had found in the fighting that Quandrix mathematics and Witherbloom growth made something new, which is the card {Zimone and Dina}.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "mom-ravnica-and-zendikar", "Ravnica and Zendikar",
                    listOf(
                        "Vraska came home to Ravnica as a conqueror. She drove Realmbreaker into New Prahv, the Azorius guildhall, and the prison where she had once been held, and ordered Ravnicans blinded in revenge for the gorgons whose eyes had been put out. The tree heaved whole sections of the Undercity up into the air for the first time in centuries, and the compleated Golgari poured out of it. Many Izzet and Simic went over willingly, out of sheer curiosity, and learned the price too late.",
                        "The guilds fought back. Ral Zarek had felt something coming for a long time and had built a sonic weapon tuned against the oil, and with it he beat Vraska. In the fight she set off a memory reset [Jace](who/jace) had placed in her mind long ago on Ixalan, and seemed to come back to herself. When Ral's people went looking for her body, it was gone. {Invasion of Ravnica} becomes the Guildpact Paragon, all ten guilds fighting under one law.",
                        "[Nahiri](who/nahiri) led the invasion of Zendikar. She bound herself into the Emeria Skyclave and made it an engine to compleat the whole plane, compleated its elementals and even stilled the Roil. The plane answered anyway. In the wilderness the land rose against the invaders, and in the Skyclaves a band of elite defenders led by Akiri made a run at Nahiri's engine and broke it. The Skyclave fell and Sea Gate was overrun. In the same skies, the vampire bloodchief Drana and the angel Linvala found by accident that their magic fit together, and their contempt for each other became respect, the card {Drana and Linvala}.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote("Nahiri tried to bend Zendikar to Phyrexia's will. In response, the world rose against her.", "Invasion of Zendikar"),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "mom-ikoria-and-ixalan", "Ikoria and Ixalan",
                    listOf(
                        "Lukka came back to Ikoria and destroyed Drannith, the walled city that had once hunted monsters and the people bonded to them. He did not spread the oil far. He gathered Ikoria's monsters into himself instead, building a titan out of their flesh. Jirina Kudro led Drannith's survivors toward Lavabrink with Vivien Reid and the monster hunter Chevill, and when the wounded could no longer outrun him, she led them to the nest of the apex monster Vadrok. Vadrok tore Lukka out of his flesh titan and burned him, Vivien finished him, and the monster flew away and left the humans alive. {Tandem Takedown} remembers it. Across the plane, some of Ikoria's crystals bled oil, and others changed in answer and gave the monsters a defense against it.",
                        "Huatli warned the Sun Empire, and its capital Pachatupa was ready. The Phyrexians came out of the sea on submarine war machines, three hosts together, and within days tens of thousands of people and whole regiments of dinosaurs had been compleated. Atzocan, Otepec and a dozen towns along the northern barrier were wiped out. The elder dinosaur Etali was compleated, and hurricanes of red lightning and razor wind followed it, grounding every flying dinosaur.",
                        "The Sun Empire, the pirates and the vampires of the Legion of Dusk fought together for the first time. The Empire summoned the elder dinosaur Ghalta to ambush the invaders, and Mavren Fein's legionnaires followed in its wake, the pair on {Ghalta and Mavren}. Zacama brought Etali down at last, after Etali had wounded Zetalpa, and {Etali, Primal Conqueror} is the dinosaur before and after.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "mom-eldraine-and-innistrad", "Eldraine and Innistrad",
                    listOf(
                        "Eldraine's invasion began quietly in the Wilds, and nobody noticed until it was too late. Within days the courts had fallen. High King Algenus and Queen Linden were dead, Ayara had been compleated with all of Castle Locthwain, and Lake Lochmere flooded until Vantress was more moat than castle. The knights fought on as guerrillas, and the fae of the Wilds turned from pranks to deadlier tricks and guided lost travellers to safety.",
                        "Part of it was won by accident. Torbran, the dwarf lord of Embereth, rescued the faerie Rankle from a converter beast, and Rankle stole him a ring of three wishes. Rankle spent its last wish making a love potion rain over Locthwain, and every Phyrexian there fell madly in love with him and chased him into a chasm. Rankle died there, and the Phyrexians fell into an everlasting sleep, which is {Rankle and Torbran}. Afterward the fae lord Talion and three witch sisters cast the Wicked Slumber over the realm, the story told in [Wilds of Eldraine](wilds-of-eldraine).",
                        "Innistrad had survived too many ends of the world to panic. [Teferi](who/teferi) had warned Arlinn Kord to watch for mechanical creatures, and she and [Sorin](who/sorin) Markov prepared together. Humans and monsters did not join forces, and their scattered defenses still held better than almost anywhere, because the plane's zombies could not be compleated. Gisa and Geralf Cecani set their feud aside long enough to destroy a Phyrexian force at Havengul with their combined dead. Thalia, the last survivor of her cathar squad, was saved by the Gitrog Monster, which had left its lake because the machines tasted awful, and she rode it into battle on {Thalia and The Gitrog Monster}.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "mom-the-far-planes", "The Far Planes",
                    listOf(
                        "Some worlds in the war had never been seen before and appear only on their battle cards. On Xerex, the literal-minded Phyrexians made the worst mistake possible there, and tried to understand what they were seeing. On Azgol they did not know that no one may approach the Ashen Idol without an offering, and the volcanoes destroyed them. On Ergamon, predators and prey stopped hunting each other until every invader was dust. On Pyrulea the slabhorns broke the tree's branches like fern stems, and on Kylem the competitors of Valor's Reach fought the invaders as hard as they fought each other, while the referees penalized the Phyrexians for breaking the rules.",
                        "Old worlds came back too. On Shandalar the wild mana stripped the invaders' carapaces away and left the flesh beneath, and magic from the Chain Veil, seeping through from a vault on Ravnica, woke the sleeping Onakke. On Mercadia, young Kyren goblins tried to be as brave as the legendary Squee. On Ulgrotha, Grandmother Ravi Sengir led the defense and rather enjoyed the apocalypse. On Lorwyn the elves found the Phyrexians so ugly they fought beside the eyeblights they despised. On Vryn, [Jace](who/jace) made a poor commander because his own defenses against other minds got in the way, and Vryn won by overloading its mage-rings. On Moag, the oldest trees remembered ignoring Phyrexia once before and would not do it again.",
                        "Amonkhet's survivors hid and were found anyway. Hazoret set the oil alight in Phyrexian veins with her spear while Djeru's fighters cut them apart, and they found the Scarab God and the Locust God already fighting at Naktamun. Lazotep, they learned, dulled the oil, and one convert heard [Norn](who/elesh-norn)'s voice fade to a whisper as it covered him. On Alara the Maelstrom itself rose, and {Invasion of Alara} becomes Awaken the Maelstrom, a spell of every color. On Dominaria the New Coalition was ready, and when Norn defiled Serra's temples, Serra's angels came back into the world to fight.",
                    ),
                ),
            ),
            listOf(
                StorySource("Set", "March of the Machine"),
                StorySource("Story", "Arcavios: A Radiant Heart, by Evelyn Teng, Magic Story on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Story", "Ikoria: Survival of the Fittest, by Roy Graham, Magic Story on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Story", "Ravnica: One and the Same, by Alison Lührs, Magic Story on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Story", "Zendikar: Battles in the Field and in the Mind, by A. T. Greenblatt, Magic Story on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Story", "Eldraine: The Adventures of Rankle, Master of Love, by Jenna Helland, Magic Story on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Story", "Planeswalker's Guide to March of the Machine, by Emily Teng, on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki: New Phyrexia's Invasion of the Multiverse"),
            ),
        ),
        StoryArticle(
            "mom-legendary-team-ups", "Legendary Team-Ups",
            listOf(
                StoryTab(
                    "mom-unlikely-allies", "Unlikely Allies",
                    listOf(
                        "The set's answer to a Machine Legion that spoke with one voice was a messier kind of unity. Its signature legends are team-ups, two characters from the same world fighting together on a single card, often people who should never have stood side by side. Ten rare team-ups cover the ten two-color pairs, and five mythic team-ups take the three-color wedges. Five more, in the three-color shards, came in the Commander decks, and the set's own story feature on them was titled The Legendary Team-Ups of March of the Machine.",
                        "{Zurgo and Ojutai} is the one with the longest history behind it. Zurgo, the orc who had been hounded by dragons all his life, was the first on Tarkir to see that dragons and the peoples beneath them would not survive the invasion without each other. He swallowed his bitterness and his pride and went to the dragonlord most likely to listen to reason, Ojutai, and Ojutai, his dragons' numbers cut down by the invaders, agreed. The despised outcast and the dragonlord set an example, and soon dragons and their scions were fighting together across the plane as never before.",
                        "{Thalia and The Gitrog Monster} is the strangest. Life had been good for the Gitrog Monster until machines came to its lake that tasted awful and tried to hurt it. It went to Thraben, found Thalia about to be overwhelmed, and bowled through her attackers, seeing in her a new human to bring it food. Then it waited, and she understood it was offering a ride. She does not steer, and she knows that if the invaders ever run out, she might become the next meal.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "mom-old-enemies", "Old Enemies",
                    listOf(
                        "When Phyrexian attacks broke open the prison holding the former Consulate officer Baral, he escaped straight into a host of Phyrexians and would have died there if the skyship pirate Kari Zev had not pulled him out. When she realized whom she had rescued, she gave him a choice that was no choice at all, help her fight or go overboard. Her monkey, First Mate Ragavan, was set to watch Baral for any sign of treachery. Both of them knew that if they survived they would be on opposite sides again. That is {Baral and Kari Zev}, and the new Consulate later forgave Baral his old crimes for how hard he fought for the plane.",
                        "Kairi, the coolest-headed of Kamigawa's spirit dragons, knew the plane needed every scrap of help it could find, even help from a demon locked away in the spirit realm. He freed Hidetsugu, who was amused to find his rescuer was the reincarnation of the dragon he had once slain, and happy for the chance to wreak havoc. Kairi remembers that death vividly. As soon as Hidetsugu is no longer needed, Kairi means to lock him up again. The card is {Hidetsugu and Kairi}.",
                        "On Theros, the Invasion Tree reached down into the Underworld itself, and the Phyrexians marching through it met Kroxa, titan of death's hunger, who devoured them so thoroughly that not even oil was left. Athreos struck Kroxa's chains and set him loose in the Underworld, and charged the hound Kunoros with steering him along the paths where he would do the most harm to Phyrexians and the least to everyone else. That is {Kroxa and Kunoros}.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote("When the Invasion Tree reached the Underworld, it found only teeth and fury.", "Kroxa and Kunoros"),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "mom-strange-partners", "Strange Partners",
                    listOf(
                        "When Yargle, the demonic frog spirit of Urborg, saw the Phyrexians charging toward him, he was delighted. So much food, all so eager to be eaten. He did not notice that the more he ate, the more glistening oil he swallowed. Muldrotha, the Urborg elemental who is daughter to the maro-sorcerer Multani, noticed for him, and persuaded her reluctant father to help. Multani bound himself to Yargle and lent him what he knew of Phyrexia, and came away with a new appreciation of Urborg's strange ways. {Yargle and Multani} has no rules text at all, only an enormous body, and its power beat the record held by Impervious Greatwurm.",
                        "When the invasion broke through on Ravnica, the first thing the homunculus Fblthp did was run away, and somehow he ended up on the front line facing a horde of compleated Golgari. The cyclops Borborygmos, smashing Phyrexians nearby, saved him. Fblthp stayed, perched where he could squeak in fright at anything that came too close, and Borborygmos smashed whatever the tiny voice was yelling about. That is {Borborygmos and Fblthp}.",
                        "On Ikoria, the giant ape Kogla rode the spiny-backed turtle Yidaro into battle and used him as a Phyrexian perforator, and it is not clear that Yidaro ever noticed. On Eldraine, the friendship of the dwarf lord Torbran and the faerie Rankle began when Torbran saved Rankle from a converter beast, and ended when Rankle gave his life to lure the Phyrexians into a chasm. Torbran will mourn him all his days. The cards are {Kogla and Yidaro} and {Rankle and Torbran}.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "mom-gods-and-mortals", "Gods and Mortals",
                    listOf(
                        "Warned of the coming invasion, the survivors of Amonkhet went into hiding, and it did no good. Those who had lived through Nicol [Bolas](who/nicol-bolas)'s ruin of their world were tough, though, and they struck back with a plan built around their protector god Hazoret and her loyal lieutenant Djeru. At Naktamun they found the Scarab God and the Locust God already fighting. While the insect gods' undead held the Phyrexians, Hazoret set the oil in their veins alight with her flaming spear, and Djeru's fighters cut them apart as they writhed. The insect gods left for the deep desert without a word. The card is {Djeru and Hazoret}.",
                        "A year before, Vorinclex had wounded the god Esika on Kaldheim and stolen the essence of the World Tree. When the tree grown from it broke into the Cosmos, Esika lost her sense of the realms and could no longer find her way, and the other gods needed her Cosmos Elixir to stay strong and uncorrupted. She turned to the one mortal she trusted, Inga Rune-Eyes, leader of the Omenseekers, who charted safe routes for Esika's chariot through the Cosmos. That is {Inga and Esika}.",
                        "On New Capenna, Giada had set the angels' awakening in motion, and weeks later she watched over Errant while the street artist placed the charges that brought Park Heights down on Atraxa. Giada never let Errant see her, strengthened her Halo cartridges, shielded her from the worst of it, and at the end made sure one of her sisters caught Errant as the neighborhood fell. Errant never knew.",
                    ),
                ),
            ),
            listOf(
                StorySource("Set", "March of the Machine"),
                StorySource("Story", "The Legendary Team-Ups of March of the Machine, by Emily Teng, on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki: March of the Machine"),
                StorySource("Reference", "Scryfall"),
            ),
        ),
        StoryArticle(
            "mom-battles-and-mechanics", "Battles and Mechanics",
            listOf(
                StoryTab(
                    "mom-battles", "Battles",
                    listOf(
                        "March of the Machine introduced the first new permanent card type since planeswalkers, the battle. It had been teased three months before, when the rules text of {Atraxa, Grand Unifier} in All Will Be One listed battle among the card types, the same trick Future Sight once played to hint at planeswalkers. Every draft and set booster carried one, and the war each battle stands for is told in [its own tabs](mom-the-invasion-begins) on this page.",
                        "Battles are double-faced cards printed sideways, and they were the first permanent ever to sit on the battlefield turned on its side. You cast one when you could cast a sorcery. It enters with defense counters equal to the number printed in its corner, and every point of damage dealt to it takes one away. All thirty-six battles in the set carry the subtype Siege. As a Siege enters, its controller chooses an opponent to protect it. Anyone else may attack it, its protector blocks for it, and when the last defense counter is removed, its controller exiles it and casts the other face for free.",
                        "The idea was that the players were fighting in the invasion, liberating a world. The front of each battle shows a plane under attack, and the back is the reward for freeing it, often that world's own champion, like {Invasion of Tarkir} turning into Defiant Thundermaw or {Invasion of Ikoria} into Zilortha. There is one battle for every major setting Magic had visited, plus a few new worlds. {Invasion of Ravnica} and {Invasion of Alara} are a matched pair for the first two planes built around many-colored factions. The Ravnica battle is colorless and its back rewards two-color spells, and the Alara battle costs one of every color and becomes a spell that is every color. Mark Rosewater has said battles are now a tool R&D uses where they fit, not a fixture of every set.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "mom-incubate", "Incubate",
                    listOf(
                        "The Phyrexians' own mechanic is incubate. To incubate a number, you create an Incubator, an artifact token carrying that many +1/+1 counters, which can later be paid two mana to transform into a Phyrexian artifact creature that keeps all the counters. It is a seedpod waiting to hatch. The token is double-faced, and March of the Machine was the first set with transforming double-faced tokens at all.",
                        "Some of the set's strongest cards incubate. {Sunfall} exiles every creature on the battlefield and then incubates once for each creature it exiled, leaving its caster a single huge Phyrexian. {Chrome Host Seedshark} incubates every time its owner casts a noncreature spell, the size of the spell. {Glissa, Herald of Predation} offers a choice each combat, to incubate twice, to hatch every Incubator, or to give her Phyrexians first strike and deathtouch. {Progenitor Exarch} makes a whole clutch of them when it arrives, and can hatch one each turn.",
                        "Like amass and Zombies in War of the Spark, incubate came with support for its creature type, and many cards care how many Phyrexians their owner controls. In draft, white and black played Phyrexians matter, and black and green played incubate. The flavor matched the story's seedpods exactly. A drafter who passed on dealing with an Incubator often found out two mana later what it had been carrying.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "mom-compleated-cards", "Compleated Cards",
                    listOf(
                        "The set used transforming double-faced cards to show compleation as it happened. A front face shows someone from one of the invaded worlds, and an activated ability paid partly in Phyrexian mana turns them into a Phyrexian on the back, now two colors. There is a cycle of these at common and two at uncommon. The commons pull in the neighboring color on the color wheel, and the uncommons pull in an enemy color.",
                        "At rare, five compleated generals each recall a famous Phyrexian card of the past. {Heliod, the Radiant Dawn} becomes a Phyrexian god that echoes Consecrated Sphinx. {Rona, Herald of Invasion} becomes a Tolarian Obliterator that echoes Phyrexian Obliterator. {Ayara, Widow of the Realm} echoes Geth, {Etali, Primal Conqueror} echoes Blightsteel Colossus, and {Polukranos Reborn} echoes Wurmcoil Engine.",
                        "The five praetors themselves, [Elesh Norn](who/elesh-norn) and the four she ruled or broke, are mythic creatures whose backs are sagas. {Elesh Norn} transforms into The Argent Etchings, {Jin-Gitaxias} into The Great Synthesis, {Sheoldred} into The True Scriptures, {Urabrask} into The Great Work and {Vorinclex} into The Grand Evolution. Each saga's final chapter is built like a planeswalker's ultimate ability, and it turns the saga back into the praetor. Blue and green in draft played transformation matters, built around all of these.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "mom-backup-and-convoke", "Backup and Convoke",
                    listOf(
                        "Where the Phyrexians incubated, the defenders backed each other up. Backup is a creature keyword with a number. When the creature enters, it puts that many +1/+1 counters on a target creature, and if that target is another creature, the target also gains the backup creature's other abilities until end of turn. {Boon-Bringer Valkyrie} lends flying, first strike and lifelink to whoever she arrives beside. {Doomskar Warrior} lends trample and the power to dig for creatures and lands when it hits a player or a battle. Red and white built a whole draft archetype around it.",
                        "Convoke returned, letting creatures tap to help pay for spells, which suited a set about whole worlds pulling together. {Zephyr Singer} gives a flying counter to every creature that helped cast it, and {Ancient Imperiosaur} gets counters for each one. Blue and red drafted it. Basic landcycling came back to Standard for the first time since Conflux, and kicker became a mechanic that would come back often.",
                        "The ten draft pairs were Knights in white and blue, the graveyard in blue and black, sacrifice in black and red, battles in red and green, +1/+1 counters in green and white, Phyrexians in white and black, convoke in blue and red, incubate in black and green, backup in red and white, and transformation in green and blue. Each had a gold uncommon signpost and a gold uncommon battle to point the way.",
                    ),
                ),
            ),
            listOf(
                StorySource("Set", "March of the Machine"),
                StorySource("Story", "March of the Machine Mechanics, by Matt Tabak, on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Story", "March of the Machine Learning, Parts 1 and 2, by Mark Rosewater, on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki: Battle"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki: Incubate"),
                StorySource("Reference", "Scryfall"),
            ),
        ),
        StoryArticle(
            "mom-cards-worth-knowing", "Cards Worth Knowing",
            listOf(
                StoryTab(
                    "mom-the-praetor-cards", "The Praetor Cards",
                    listOf(
                        "Every praetor got a last card in the set where they died. {Elesh Norn} is a four mana creature with vigilance that taxes every source an opponent controls, costing its controller two life each time it deals damage to her owner or their permanents unless they pay one mana, and she can sacrifice three other creatures to become her saga. {Sheoldred} makes each opponent sacrifice a creature or planeswalker when she arrives. {Jin-Gitaxias} draws a card for every expensive noncreature spell. {Urabrask} pings an opponent and adds red mana with every instant or sorcery. {Vorinclex} fetches two Forests.",
                        "{Realmbreaker, the Invasion Tree} ties them together. For ten mana it can be sacrificed to put any number of Praetor cards from its owner's library onto the battlefield at once, which is the closest any card comes to the invasion's opening moment. [Elesh Norn](who/elesh-norn) herself also appears in the bonus sheet in her old form, which is in the Multiverse Legends tab here.",
                        "The story's other key players got cards too. {Wrenn and Realmbreaker} makes every land tap for any color, animates lands into 3/3 Elementals, and has an ultimate that lets its owner play lands and permanents from the graveyard. {Archangel Elspeth} makes lifelinking soldiers, turns a creature into an angel, and can bring back every small permanent from the graveyard. {Invasion of New Phyrexia} creates a Knight for every point of X and flips into Teferi Akosa of Zhalfir, the planeswalker [Teferi](who/teferi) at the head of his army.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "mom-constructed-staples", "Constructed Staples",
                    listOf(
                        "Several cards from the set became staples well beyond Standard. {Sunfall} became one of the standard sweepers of its era. {Faerie Mastermind} is the card of Yuta Takahashi, world champion, a flash flier that draws whenever an opponent draws their second card in a turn, and it found a home in older formats. {Invasion of Tarkir} is burn that rewards holding dragons, and {Invasion of Ikoria} is a tutor that puts a creature straight onto the battlefield. {Chandra, Hope's Beacon} gave [Chandra](who/chandra) a six mana planeswalker card that copies spells and adds mana, a fitting card for the walker who went back into New Phyrexia when everyone else went home.",
                        "Two cards finished cycles that had been waiting for years. {Omnath, Locus of All} is the five-color Omnath, completing the cycle of elemental Omnaths. {Sword of Once and Future} is the blue and black Mirran sword, giving protection from those colors and letting its creature recast cheap spells from the graveyard, and it completed the long cycle of swords that began on Mirrodin.",
                        "A few cards made smaller history. {Monastery Mentor} came back in a premier set. {Yargle and Multani} was the first vanilla creature printed in a premier set in over two years, with more power than any creature printed before it. The set's common gainlands each show the invasion of a different world, and their flavor text tells a line of the war on each one. And this was the set that moved collector numbers to four digits and dropped the old fraction printed in the corner.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "mom-commander-favorites", "Commander Favorites",
                    listOf(
                        "Commander players took to the set's legends. {Thalia and The Gitrog Monster} gives its owner an extra land drop each turn, makes opponents' creatures and nonbasic lands enter tapped, and draws a card every time it attacks at the cost of a creature or land. {Etali, Primal Conqueror} reveals a card from every player's library on arrival and lets its owner cast all of them free. {Glissa, Herald of Predation} and {Chrome Host Seedshark} anchor whole incubate decks.",
                        "The team-ups made commanders out of pairs nobody expected. {Kroxa and Kunoros} can exile five cards from its owner's graveyard to bring a creature back every time it enters or attacks. {Zimone and Dina}, {Inga and Esika}, {Borborygmos and Fblthp} and {Yargle and Multani} all lead decks built around them, the last as a joke that sometimes wins. The battles found a place too, especially {Invasion of Alara}, which casts a free spell the moment it enters.",
                        "{Realmbreaker, the Invasion Tree} is a favorite in decks built around the praetors, and {Polukranos Reborn} and the other compleated generals reward the Phyrexian mana that lets any deck pay life to transform them. The set's Commander decks, in their own tab here, added more legends on top of all these.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "mom-multiverse-legends", "Multiverse Legends",
                    listOf(
                        "The set had a bonus sheet called Multiverse Legends, code MUL. It held sixty-five reprinted legendary creatures, each in the special frame of its home world, the Ikoria comic book frame, the Theros constellation frame, the Kaladesh filigree frame and so on, with Phyrexian legends in a frame of ichor. Every draft and set booster held one, and collector boosters held at least three. The cards were legal only where they already were legal, plus Historic on Arena.",
                        "The rule for choosing them was that each had to be a character still alive and able to be fighting in the war. The Phyrexian praetors appear in their old forms, {Elesh Norn, Grand Cenobite}, {Sheoldred, Whispering One}, {Jin-Gitaxias, Core Augur}, {Urabrask the Hidden} and {Vorinclex, Voice of Hunger}, along with {Atraxa, Praetors' Voice}. Defenders appear beside them, {Thalia, Guardian of Thraben}, {Niv-Mizzet Reborn}, {Squee, the Immortal}, {Kenrith, the Returned King}, {Lurrus of the Dream-Den} and many more.",
                        "The most valuable was {Ragavan, Nimble Pilferer}, the monkey who also appears as Kari Zev's first mate in the team-ups. It was banned in Historic on Arena five days before the set was even released. Earlier bonus sheets had reprinted spells and artifacts, the Mystical Archive of Strixhaven and the Retro Artifacts of The Brothers' War, and this one did the same for legends. The next set, Wilds of Eldraine, followed with a bonus sheet of its own called Enchanting Tales.",
                    ),
                ),
            ),
            listOf(
                StorySource("Set", "March of the Machine"),
                StorySource("Set", "Multiverse Legends"),
                StorySource("Story", "Collecting March of the Machine, by Max McCall and Adam Styborski, on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki: March of the Machine/Multiverse Legends"),
                StorySource("Reference", "Scryfall"),
            ),
        ),
        StoryArticle(
            "mom-commander-decks", "Commander Decks",
            listOf(
                StoryTab(
                    "mom-five-decks", "Five Decks",
                    listOf(
                        "Five Commander decks came out beside the set, each a hundred cards, each with its own new cards and reprints, and each in three colors. Growing Threat was white and black, led by {Brimaz, Blight of Oreskos}, the compleated leonin king who incubates whenever his owner casts a Phyrexian or artifact creature and proliferates when a Phyrexian dies. Cavalry Charge was white, blue and black, Knights led by {Sidar Jabari of Zhalfir}, a flying commander who loots whenever Knights attack, even from the command zone.",
                        "Call for Backup was red, green and white, built on +1/+1 counters and led by {Bright-Palm, Soul Awakener}, a commander with backup that doubles the counters on a creature each time it attacks. Divine Convocation was blue, red and white, built on convoke and led by the angel {Kasla, the Broken Halo}, who draws a card every time her owner casts another convoke spell. Tinker Time was green, blue and red, all artifact tokens, led by {Gimbal, Gremlin Prodigy}, who gives artifact creatures trample and builds a new Gremlin each end step.",
                        "Each deck had more team-ups of its own. {Moira and Teshar}, {Elenda and Azor}, {Shalai and Hallar}, {Saint Traft and Rem Karolus} and {Rashmi and Ragavan} are the five team-ups in the three-color shards, finishing the mega cycle the set's wedge mythics began. Growing Threat also brought a new Phyrexian giant, {Blight Titan}, and every one of the five decks carried a new artifact, {Ichor Elixir}.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "mom-planechase-returns", "Planechase Returns",
                    listOf(
                        "The decks brought back Planechase, the variant where players share a deck of planes and roll a planar die to travel between them. Every deck included ten planar cards, five new planes, four reprinted planes and one reprinted phenomenon. It was the first new Planechase product in years, and it suited a set about a war fought across every world at once.",
                        "The planes in the decks visit worlds from across the Multiverse, Strixhaven and Ghirapur, Towashi and Littjara, Valor's Reach and the Golden City of Orazca among them. The darkest was {Norn's Seedcore}. Whenever chaos ensues there, its owner reveals planes until another plane appears and travels to it without leaving, so the Seedcore piles world on world on the table the way Realmbreaker pierced them. [Elesh Norn](who/elesh-norn) would have liked it.",
                        "Each deck also had a sorcery from the Paths cycle, {Path of the Schemer} among them, with a new ability called Will of the Planeswalkers. After the spell does its work, every player votes to planeswalk or for chaos to ensue, and the planar deck moves either way. They work in any Commander game, and are at their best in one with a planar deck on the table.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "mom-the-talents", "The Talents",
                    listOf(
                        "Eight cards with the Commander set code were never in the decks at all. They were only found in the main set's set and collector boosters, and they were legal only in Commander, Legacy and Vintage. Five of them were the Talents, a cycle of enchantments that attach to a planeswalker and give it a loyalty ability from that planeswalker's first expansion card, plus a new static ability.",
                        "{Teferi's Talent} grants an ability that makes an emblem letting its owner activate loyalty abilities any time they could cast an instant, and puts a loyalty counter on the planeswalker whenever they draw. {Elspeth's Talent} reaches back to Elspeth, Sun's Champion. {Liliana's Talent}, {Rowan's Talent} and {Vivien's Talent} finish the cycle, and every one of them works on any planeswalker, not just its namesake. The five namesakes, [Teferi](who/teferi), Elspeth, [Liliana](who/liliana), Rowan and Vivien, all fought somewhere in the war.",
                        "The other three booster cards were {Begin the Invasion}, which puts X battles straight onto the battlefield, {Infernal Sovereign} and {Firemane Commando}. Prerelease packs held three more team-ups as promos, {Goro-Goro and Satoru}, {Katilda and Lier} and {Slimefoot and Squee}, legal in Commander but not in the set's own prerelease decks.",
                    ),
                ),
            ),
            listOf(
                StorySource("Set", "March of the Machine Commander"),
                StorySource("Story", "March of the Machine Commander Decklists, on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki: March of the Machine/Commander decks"),
                StorySource("Reference", "Scryfall"),
            ),
        ),
        StoryArticle(
            "mom-the-aftermath", "The Aftermath",
            listOf(
                StoryTab(
                    "mom-the-epilogue", "The Epilogue",
                    listOf(
                        "The Aftermath came out on May 12, 2023, three weeks after the main set, under the code MAT. It was a micro set of fifty cards, fifteen uncommons, twenty-five rares and ten mythics, with a hundred and eighty alternate versions. It was legal in Standard and could not be drafted. Mark Rosewater called it a new kind of thing and not a normal expansion, and Wizards sold it as an extension of March of the Machine.",
                        "It was the story's epilogue. The Phyrexians were beaten, but the Multiverse had changed. Most planeswalkers had lost their sparks, and the scars Realmbreaker left in the world had become [the Omenpaths](the-omenpaths), doors between planes that anyone could walk through. Who lost a spark and who kept one is told in [Sparks Lost](sparks-lost). The cards show the survivors rebuilding on their home worlds, and many of the planeswalkers return as plain legendary creatures marked with a cracked planeswalker symbol.",
                        "Only two stories came with it, She Who Breaks the World by Grace Fong and Beyond Repair by Emily Teng, and the set had no Story Spotlight cards and little flavor text, so many threads were left unexplained. Its expansion symbol is a planeswalker's spark.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote("Phyrexia was defeated, but the repercussions of the invasion rippled through the Blind Eternities.", "Spark Rupture"),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "mom-she-who-breaks", "She Who Breaks the World",
                    listOf(
                        "On Zhalfir, Nissa Revane could not hear the plane. Her spark was gone, as were Teferi's and Koth's, and [Chandra](who/chandra) was one of the few who kept hers. [Karn](who/karn) and Koth roofed houses for the Mirran refugees, [Teferi](who/teferi) had his country back, and Nissa buried her Phyrexian carapace alone, ashamed that part of her missed hearing every plane through the tree. Chandra melted the carapace for her, and then told her she was leaving to find Ajani, who had gone off alone. She had kissed Nissa, and now she was going. Nissa asked what kind of love Chandra had for her, and Chandra could not say, and left.",
                        "Nissa tried to meditate beside the sprout that had been Wrenn, and the static of every mind that had ever touched hers, the Eldrazi, [Bolas](who/nicol-bolas) and loudest of all Phyrexia, drowned out the land. A tear opened in the air beside her, and a storm beast came out of it, a creature from some other plane. Teferi, Karn and Koth saved her the first time. When it came back to the village, Nissa led it away alone, and Chandra came back in time to save her and nearly set the savanna on fire.",
                        "Trapped together in a hole, Nissa finally told her the truth, that being left behind without a spark had hurt her. Chandra apologized and asked what to do. Nissa sorted the noise in her head into the songs of every plane she had known, sang them together to Zhalfir and was answered, and they gave the homesick storm elemental the heat it needed to become rain again. That night they stood before the portal on the hill, where Nissa thought she could faintly hear Zendikar. Chandra said she would walk beside her, and they stepped through together.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote("When Nissa tapped into Zhalfir's leylines, she was shocked to feel not a piece of Dominaria, but a plane all its own.", "Leyline Immersion"),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "mom-beyond-repair", "Beyond Repair",
                    listOf(
                        "Deep in the wreck of the Emeria Skyclave, [Nahiri](who/nahiri) ripped Phyrexia out of her plane one handspan at a time. She had woken alive after the war and torn the metal out of her own body first, leaving scars in the Skyclaves' diamond pattern. Her spark was gone, and every act of lithomancy cost her now. In the chamber where she had been fused to the Skyclave she found a hedron grown in thin layers around a seed, and the seed was her spark, protected by stone while it powered the compleation engine.",
                        "She climbed into daylight for the first time since the war and saw what she had done to Zendikar. Then Ajani appeared, cleansed of Phyrexia and still a planeswalker, and asked her to help him make amends across the Multiverse, because only the two of them knew what they had done. Nahiri suspected he had come to kill her. She threw his crimes at him, the gods of Theros, Jaya Ballard, and when he snarled in grief she pulled up a wall of stone and the Skyclave's dome collapsed under her.",
                        "She fell onto the cairn of Phyrexian metal she had built. The hedron cracked, and her spark was gone for good. Ajani had vanished. Nahiri decided the real enemy was not Phyrexia. It was planeswalkers, who came to a world, broke it and left. [Sorin](who/sorin) and [Ugin](who/ugin) had bound the Eldrazi in Zendikar ages ago, and now this. She swore that no planeswalker would ever set foot on Zendikar again.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote("The relic shattered, and something in Nahiri broke as well.", "Reckless Handling"),
                ),
            ),
            listOf(
                StorySource("Set", "March of the Machine: The Aftermath"),
                StorySource("Story", "March of the Machine: The Aftermath, She Who Breaks the World, by Grace Fong, Magic Story on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Story", "March of the Machine: The Aftermath, Beyond Repair, by Emily Teng, Magic Story on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki: March of the Machine: The Aftermath"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki: Desparkening"),
            ),
        ),
        StoryArticle(
            "mom-aftermath-cards", "Aftermath Cards",
            listOf(
                StoryTab(
                    "mom-the-sparkless", "The Sparkless",
                    listOf(
                        "The heart of The Aftermath is planeswalkers who are planeswalkers no longer, printed as legendary creatures with a cracked planeswalker symbol as their watermark. {Karn, Legacy Reforged} is an artifact creature whose size equals the largest artifact its owner controls, and who makes colorless mana for every artifact. {Nissa, Resurgent Animist} adds mana with every land and digs for Elves and Elementals. {Nahiri, Forged in Fury} casts Equipment free from the top of her owner's library. [Karn](who/karn) and [Nahiri](who/nahiri) are both here as what the war left of them.",
                        "Others show where the war left people. {Ob Nixilis, Captive Kingpin} is stranded on New Capenna without a spark, facing five families out for blood. {Sarkhan, Soul Aflame} copies the dragons he can no longer become at will. {Narset, Enlightened Exile}, {Calix, Guided by Fate}, {Kiora, Sovereign of the Deep}, {Samut, Vizier of Naktamun} and {Tyvar the Bellicose} round out the desparked. {Samut, Vizier of Naktamun} is the first creature ever printed with exactly first strike, vigilance and haste.",
                        "Two enchantments speak to the change directly. {Spark Rupture} turns every planeswalker into a creature with power and toughness equal to its loyalty, and was the bundle promo. {Deification} protects the planeswalkers of one chosen type from dying to damage while their owner controls a creature, rather like an old card called Worship. {Leyline Immersion} enchants a legendary creature and lets it tap for five mana, and {The Kenriths' Royal Funeral} mourns the High King and Queen of Eldraine.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "mom-rebuilding", "Rebuilding",
                    listOf(
                        "The rest of the set is the Multiverse cleaning up. On Ravnica, {Undercity Upheaval} shows light on parts of the Undercity for the first time in centuries, and {Niv-Mizzet, Supreme} reclaims his place as the plane's highest authority. On Kaladesh, {Pia Nalaar, Consul of Revival} is a renegade turned revolutionary turned rebuilder. On Ikoria, {Jirina, Dauntless General} leads survivors through {Drannith Ruins}, a city left a playground for monsters. On Ixalan, Huatli built {Gold-Forged Thopteryx}, a device Saheeli would have been proud of.",
                        "On Dominaria, {Danitha, New Benalia's Light} had spent her life preparing to lead House Capashen and mourned that the chance came so soon. On Kamigawa, {Nashi, Moon's Legacy} carries on his mother's stories. On Zhalfir, {Jolrael, Voice of Zhalfir} was the Buy-a-Box promo. On Capenna, {Metropolis Reformer} shows the angels, their ancient enemy gone, turning their eyes on their former captors, the demons. {Feast of the Victorious Dead} shows survivors telling it as if they had beaten [Elesh Norn](who/elesh-norn) bare-handed.",
                        "{Filter Out} purges the last of the inert oil, {Rebuild the City} turns Phyrexian wreckage into building material, and {Campus Renovation} shows Lorehold students rebuilding Strixhaven, reconstructing the past being their specialty. {Training Grounds} was the set's only reprint. Returning mechanics appear only once each, constellation, convoke, jump-start, landfall, madness and undergrowth, and The Aftermath is the only Magic set ever printed in which no two cards form a cycle.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "mom-a-failed-experiment", "A Failed Experiment",
                    listOf(
                        "Epilogue Boosters held five cards each, one to three rares or mythics and the rest uncommons, with a guaranteed foil and showcase card. There were Epilogue Collector Boosters and a bundle too, and the set was printed in English and Japanese only. On Arena it replaced the usual Alchemy release and appeared in a limited draft alongside the main set. The whole card list leaked before release.",
                        "Players did not like it. The set was small, the story felt thin, the boosters were expensive for what they held, and it could not be drafted. Mark Rosewater later said it had the lowest top two box score ever recorded for a randomized booster product, with only five percent of surveyed players rating it in the top two boxes.",
                        "The consequences were real. Wizards cancelled an epilogue set already designed for Outlaws of Thunder Junction and folded many of its cards into that set as The Big Score bonus sheet, and changed how later boosters of its kind were built. The Epilogue Booster as a product was cancelled for good. The story it told, of sparks lost and doors opened, went on into every set after it, starting with [Wilds of Eldraine](wilds-of-eldraine).",
                    ),
                ),
            ),
            listOf(
                StorySource("Set", "March of the Machine: The Aftermath"),
                StorySource("Story", "Doing the Aftermath, by Mark Rosewater, on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Story", "Odds and Ends 2023, Part 2, by Mark Rosewater, on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki: March of the Machine: The Aftermath"),
                StorySource("Reference", "Scryfall"),
            ),
        ),
    ),
)
